{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Who are the men, etc.</b> One needs to know why HaShem asked him — is anything withheld from Him? Further, one needs to know why it was necessary to say the word <b>with you</b> and the phrase <b>these men</b> did not suffice? Further, one needs to know Balaam's answer, in which he said ״בָּלָק בֶּן צִפּוֹר״ etc. — was His, blessed be He, question who sent them? His question was who they are! And the Sages of blessed memory sensed this difficulty and said (Yalkut Shimoni 752) — and this is their language: ״מִי הָאֲנָשִׁים״ etc. — that wicked man said: I know nothing of them except that Balak son of Zippor sent to me — end quote. Meaning, from the very words he said, ״בָּלָק etc. sent״ etc., it is understood that he intended to say ״I do not know etc. except that Balak sent״ etc. Further, one needs to know why it was necessary for him to say ״king of Moab״ and mentioning his name alone did not suffice. And it appears that the intent of HaShem's statement is based on what we explained regarding the verse ״לִינוּ פֹה״ — that they lodged with him in his private chamber. Therefore the statement of the Blessed One came and said to him <b>Who</b> etc. — an expression of lowliness, in the manner of (Exodus 3:11) ״מִי אָנֹכִי״ etc. Likewise He said <b>Who are these men</b> — whom you brought into this place with you, into the private chamber designated for you to speak with the angel of Elohim, for HaShem is insistent upon Balaam's honor among the nations since he is a prophet over them — just as we find (Numbers Rabbah 20:14) that He killed the she-ass because of Balaam's honor. And see the verse that follows this one: ״וַיֹּאמֶר אֱלֹהִים״. And Balaam understood His words and gave the reason why he showed them this honor: it is because they are envoys of a king — and that is his saying <b>Balak son of Zippor</b>, meaning, the one known to the prince of Midian, and moreover that he is <b>king of Moab, sent to me</b>, meaning, these men — and for the honor of royalty I lodged them with me. And with this all the difficulties are resolved." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend in His saying <b>king of Moab</b>, that this nation was not given to Israel, and he was cunning with this statement so that HaShem would be favorable to sending him and would not prevent him — and therefore He would not prevent his going with his envoys."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "The second difficulty in the Hebrew ('why necessary to say the word עמך and not suffice with the phrase האנשים האלה') reads somewhat elliptically; translated hyper-literally as it stands."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:12:19.536418299Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}